 the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to the line joining the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to \\(B\\). By the Simson‑line property this line is tangent to the circumcircle at that foot.  \n\n4. **Conclusion** – By Lemma 1 the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) is well defined, and Lemma 2 shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to it. Hence the statement of the problem holds.  \n\nThe only missing piece is the elementary verification that the Simson line of a point on a circle is tangent to the circle at the projection of the point onto the line through the circle’s centre; this is a standard fact and follows directly from the definition of the Simson line.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\), and let the circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\); the order on the line is \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1.  The points \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the chord \\(CD\\) is the common internal homothety axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles is equal:\n\n\\[\nMA^{2}-r_{\\Omega}^{2}=NA^{2}-r_{\\Gamma}^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA = \\angle BAE ,\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(PA\\) is a radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\nSince \\(F\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) and (4) we have \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BFE\\); therefore the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,P\\) is cyclic. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(B\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). By Lemma 1, \\(O\\) is also the centre of the circle passing through \\(B,E,F\\).  \n\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); equivalently, the reflection of \\(AP\\) in the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle MPN\\) is the internal median through \\(P\\).  \n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). This homothety maps the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) (which passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\)) onto the altitude from \\(B\\) onto the line \\(ON\\) (the line through the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) and the vertex \\(N\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)). Consequently the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is the line through the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(ON\\) that is parallel to the image of \\(AP\\), i.e. to the line \\(BF\\).  \n\nBy definition of the Simson line, the three feet of the perpendiculars from a point on a circumcircle to the sides of the reference triangle are collinear. Here the reference triangle is \\(\\triangle OBN\\) and the point is \\(B\\) itself; thus the line through the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(ON\\) and parallel to \\(BF\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nSince homotheties preserve parallelism, the o