defined (circumcenter of a degenerate triangle). The proof never checks that \\(A\\) is distinct from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).\n\n### ASSUMPTION_CHALLENGES ###\nCHALLENGE_1: **Spiral similarity fixes \\(P\\).**  \nThe argument that the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\) must map \\(P\\) to itself because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) is flawed. A spiral similarity fixing a point requires the point to lie on the center of the similarity **and** to be invariant under the rotation component. The proof never shows that the rotation angle is zero; thus \\(\\mathcal S(P)\\) could be a different point on the circle centred at \\(A\\) with radius \\(PA\\).\n\nCHALLENGE_2: **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEPF\\).**  \nThe proof asserts \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA\\) and then \\(\\angle BPA = \\angle BAE\\) without justification. In fact, \\(\\angle BPA\\) is not generally equal to \\(\\angle BAE\\); the equality would require \\(AB\\) to be a diameter of both circles or some other special symmetry, which is not given.\n\nCHALLENGE_3: **\\(AP\\) as a symmedian.**  \nThe claim “\\(PA\\perp MN\\) ⇒ \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)” is incorrect. A symmedian is defined via the isogonal conjugate of a median; perpendicularity to the opposite side does **not** characterize a symmedian. The proof never shows that \\(AP\\) is isogonal to the median from \\(P\\).\n\nCHALLENGE_4: **Miquel point identification.**  \nThe proof identifies the Miquel point of the quadrilateral formed by lines \\(MN,\\ \\Omega,\\ \\Gamma,\\ AP\\) with the common intersection \\(B\\). However, the Miquel point is the unique point lying on the circumcircles of the four triangles formed by the three lines taken two at a time. There is no argument that \\(B\\) satisfies this property; in fact, the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) already pass through \\(B\\), but the other two circles (through \\(M,N\\) and \\(A\\) with \\(B\\)) are not shown to pass through \\(B\\).\n\nCHALLENGE_5