\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll angles are directed and taken modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{B}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(M)=N . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 –  \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) the similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the chord \\(AC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BAD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\), triangle \\(APC\\) is isosceles and  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle PAC . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) to (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFP=\\angle FPD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow (2) gives \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle BDP\\) (both subtend the same chord \\(BP\\) in the circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\)).  \nTogether with (3) and (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BPE=\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\angle BPE=\\angle BFE\\) and the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) lie on a common circle. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 –  The reflected orthocenter lies on \\((BEF)\\) and \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(PA=PC\\); thus \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\).  \nSince \\(C,D\\) belong to the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\nPA\\perp MN . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AP\\) is the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nLet \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) be the feet of the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) onto the opposite sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe three feet \\(A,X,Y\\) lie on the *pedal circle* \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the nine‑point circle).  \nDenote by \\(O\\) the centre of \\(\\omega\\); then \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with its orthocentre \\(H\\).\n\nReflect \\(H\\) in the line \\(MN\\) and call the image \\(H'\\).  \nBecause the reflection in \\(MN\\) sends the altitude \\(AP\\) onto itself, we have  \n\n\\[\nHH'\\perp MN\\quad\\text{and}\\quad HH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(A,X,Y\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\); therefore the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\) is precisely \\(H'\\).  \nThus \\(H'\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 the circle \\((BEF)\\) coincides with \\(\\omega\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (BEF) . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we also have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma 3 –  The line through the orthocenter parallel to an altitude is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from a point \\(X\\) on a circle \\(\\mathcal C\\) to the line joining \\(X\\) with the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nThe three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the sides of any triangle inscribed in \\(\\mathcal C\\) are collinear; this line is the *Simson line* of \\(X\\) with respect to that triangle.  \nSince \\(OT\\) is a radius and \\(XT\\perp OT\\), the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\(\\mathcal C\\) at \\(T\\); consequently the Simson line passes through \\(T\\) and is tangent to \\(\\mathcal C\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nApply this fact to the triangle whose vertices are the three feet \\(A,X,Y\\) of the altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nIts circum‑circle is \\(\\omega\\).  \nThe orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(H\\); the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\) (Lemma 2).  \nBecause \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\), the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is precisely \\(HH'\\).  \nBy the property proved above, this Simson line is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(OA\\), i.e. at the point \\(H'\\).  \n\nSince \\(\\omega=(BEF)\\) (Lemma 2), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nLemma 1 gives the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,P\\).  \nLemma 2 identifies the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) as the line \\(HH'\\) where \\(H'\\) lies on the same circle \\((BEF)\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that this line is tangent to that circle.  \n\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
