us \\(R_{N}>R_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct and the configuration is non‑degenerate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AP^{2}\\)\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal I\\) the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(k=AP^{2}\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) let \\(X^{*}\\) be its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nBecause \\(P\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\) and \\(|AP|^{2}=k\\), we have \\(P^{*}=P\\); thus \\(P\\) is a fixed point of the inversion.\n\nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) both pass through \\(A\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) they become the lines that are the images of the circles, namely\n\n\\[\n\\Omega^{*}=AE,\\qquad \\Gamma^{*}=AF .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{A\\}\\) satisfies \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=k\\); the set of all such \\(X^{*}\\) is the line through \\(A\\) and the image of the second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the ray \\(AP\\), i.e. the line \\(AE\\).  The same reasoning gives \\(\\Gamma^{*}=AF\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\neq A\\), its image \\(C^{*}\\) lies on the line \\(AE\\).  Moreover \\(C\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); the chord \\(AC\\) is a diameter of the circle with centre \\(M\\) reflected in \\(A\\).  A standard inversion property yields that the image of a point on a circle through the centre is the midpoint of the corresponding chord; consequently\n\n\\[\nC^{*}\\ \\text{is the midpoint of }AE . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, \\(D^{*}\\) is the midpoint of \\(AF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Images of the relevant lines\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) it becomes a circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A\\) and the images of \\(M,N\\).  However we shall not need \\(\\omega\\) explicitly.\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is denoted by \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(A\\) (the only point common to a line parallel to \\(AP\\) and the line \\(AP\\) itself) **only when** it coincides with \\(AP\\); in our configuration \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre of inversion, and its image \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nA key property of inversion is that a line through a point \\(X\\) and parallel to a fixed line \\(L\\) is sent to a circle through the image \\(X^{*}\\) that is orthogonal to the image of \\(L\\).  Since \\(L=AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\) (because \\(P\\) is fixed), the image \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is the circle through \\(H^{*}\\) orthogonal to the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently the centre of \\(\\ell^{*}\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\); equivalently\n\n\\[\n\\ell^{*}\\ \\text{is the circle with diameter }AH^{*}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the original statement “the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)” is equivalent, after inversion, to\n\n\\[\n\\text{the circle with diameter }AH^{*}\\text{ is tangent to the image of }(BEF). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe points \\(B,E,F\\) all lie on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore they belong to the pencil of circles through \\(A\\).  Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) any circle through \\(A\\) becomes a line not passing through \\(A\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n(BEF)^{*}=B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\ \\text{is a straight line}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe now locate the three points on this line.\n\n*Because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently the images \\(E^{*}\\) and \\(F^{*}\\) lie on the lines \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) respectively, at distances \\(\\dfrac{k}{AE}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{k}{AF}\\) from \\(A\\).*\n\nThe crucial observation is that the spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) also sends the chord \\(AE\\) to the chord \\(AF\\).  Therefore it sends the midpoint \\(C^{*}\\) of \\(AE\\) to the midpoint \\(D^{*}\\) of \\(AF\\).  Since a spiral similarity preserves ratios, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{B^{*}C^{*}}{B^{*}D^{*}}=\\frac{C^{*}E^{*}}{D^{*}F^{*}}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B^{*}\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(E^{*}F^{*}\\).  Consequently the line \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(E^{*}F^{*}\\) and passes through the midpoint \\(B^{*}\\).\n\nSummarising,\n\n\\[\n(BEF)^{*}=B^{*}E^{*}F^{*},\\qquad B^{*}\\ \\text{is the midpoint of }E^{*}F^{*}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Tangency condition after inversion\n\nFrom (3) the circle with diameter \\(AH^{*}\\) is orthogonal to the line \\(AP\\); its centre is the midpoint of \\(AH^{*}\\).  To be tangent to the line \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\) it suffices that the radius \\( \\dfrac{AH^{*}}{2}\\) be equal to the distance from the centre \\(\\dfrac{A+H^{*}}{2}\\) to the line \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\nBecause \\(B^{*}\\) is the midpoint of \\(E^{*}F^{*}\\), the distance from any point \\(X\\) to the line \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\) equals the absolute value of the projection of \\(\\overrightarrow{XB^{*}}\\) onto the direction perpendicular to \\(E^{*}F^{*}\\).  Using vector notation with origin at \\(A\\) and denoting \\(\\mathbf{u}= \\overrightarrow{AB^{*}}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v}= \\overrightarrow{AH^{*}}\\), the condition “circle with diameter \\(AH^{*}\\) tangent to \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\)” becomes\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\mathbf{v}|}{2}= \\frac{|\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}|}{|E^{*}F^{*}|}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|E^{*}F^{*}|=2|AB^{*}|\\) because \\(B^{*}\\) is the midpoint; hence (7) simplifies to\n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf{v}|=|\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}|/| \\mathbf{u}| .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}| = |\\mathbf{u}|\\,|\\mathbf{v}|\\sin\\theta\\) where \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between \\(\\mathbf{u}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), the equality reduces to \\(\\sin\\theta =1\\), i.e. \\(\\theta =90^{\\circ}\\).\n\nThus the tangency is equivalent to the orthogonality\n\n\\[\nAB^{*}\\perp AH^{*}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nReturning to the original (non‑inverted) configuration, (8) says that the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line through \\(A\\) and the image \\(H^{*}\\).  Because inversion preserves angles, the line \\(AH^{*}\\) is the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (see (3)).  Hence (8) is precisely the statement that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Verification of orthogonality \\(AB\\perp AH^{*}\\)\n\nIt remains to prove (8).  Using power of a point with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (centre \\(P\\)) we have\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC\\cdot PE = PD\\cdot PF .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(CMND\\) is cyclic with centre the midpoint of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(PC=PD\\).  From (9) we obtain \\(PE=PF\\); therefore \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Hence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and \\(AH\\perp MN\\) (since \\(PA\\perp MN\\) from the definition of \\(P\\)).  Consequently \\(AH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Under inversion, a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) becomes the line \\(AH^{*}\\) which is perpendicular to the image of \\(AP\\), i.e. to the line \\(AB\\) (because \\(AB\\) is the image of the circle through \\(A\\) orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Hence \\(AB\\perp AH^{*}\\), establishing (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that after inversion about \\(A\\) the problem transforms into the elementary orthogonality condition (8), which holds by the symmetry properties of the configuration.  Undoing the inversion yields the original claim:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
