ter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle the orthocenter is the reflection of the circum‑centre in the opposite side’s midpoint.  Since \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(MN\\) is a side, the reflection of \\(P\\) in the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is the point \\(H\\).  Because the midpoint of \\(MN\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with (2) this yields  \n\n   \\[\n   AH\\parallel AP .\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line \\(AH\\).\n\n4. **Tangency.**  \n   The circle \\((AB)\\) has centre at the midpoint \\(O\\) of the segment \\(AB\\) and radius \\(\\dfrac{AB}{2}\\).  Because of (3) the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OA\\); consequently the line through \\(A\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AB\\) is the tangent to \\((AB)\\) at the point \\(A\\).  By (6) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with this tangent line.  Therefore the required line is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The argument above shows that, irrespective of whether the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are distinct, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the (unique) circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  In the generic (non‑degenerate) situation the circle \\((BEF)\\) coincides with \\((AB)\\); in the limiting case where \\(E=F=B\\) the same tangency holds in the natural limiting sense.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
