tinct points because  \n\n\\[\n|MN|=3<1+2.5=3.5,\\qquad |MN|>2.5-1=1.5 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)  \n\nSolving the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx^{2}+y^{2}=1,\\\\[2pt]\n(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=2.5^{2},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{25-9}{6}= \\frac{16}{6}= \\frac{8}{3}\\approx 2.666\\ldots ,\n\\qquad \ny^{2}=1-\\Bigl(\\frac{8}{3}\\Bigr)^{2}=1-\\frac{64}{9}= -\\frac{55}{9},\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Hence the above radii do **not** intersect.  \nWe therefore modify the radii slightly.\n\nTake instead  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x-0)^{2}+y^{2}=1^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=2.2^{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n|MN|=3,\\qquad 2.2-1=1.2<3<3.2=2.2+1,\n\\]\n\nso the circles intersect.  Solving  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx^{2}+y^{2}=1,\\\\[2pt]\n(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=2.2^{2}=4.84,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nsubtracting the first equation from the second yields  \n\n\\[\n(x-3)^{2}-x^{2}=3.84\\Longrightarrow x^{2}-6x+9-x^{2}=3.84\n\\Longrightarrow -6x+9=3.84,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{9-3.84}{6}= \\frac{5.16}{6}=0.86 .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=1-x^{2}=1-0.86^{2}=1-0.7396=0.2604,\n\\qquad \ny=\\pm\\sqrt{0.2604}\\approx\\pm0.510 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(0.86,\\;0.510),\\qquad B=(0.86,\\;-0.510).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AB\\) is vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (second intersections of \\(MN\\) with the circles)  \n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nIntersecting it with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the points \\((\\pm1,0)\\); the one distinct from \\(M\\) and lying on the same side of \\(M\\) as \\(N\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC=(-1,0).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the \\(x\\)-axis with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(x-3)^{2}=2.2^{2}\\Longrightarrow x-3=\\pm2.2,\n\\]\n\nhence the two points \\((0.8,0)\\) and \\((5.2,0)\\).  The one that lies beyond \\(N\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD=(5.2,0).\n\\]\n\nThus the order on the line is \\(C(-1,0),\\;M(0,0),\\;N(3,0),\\;D(5.2,0)\\), as required.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)  \n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{0.86+(-1)}{2},\\;\\frac{0.510+0}{2}\\Bigr)=\\Bigl(-0.07,\\;0.255\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand is perpendicular to the segment \\(AC\\).  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{AC}= \\frac{0.510-0}{0.86-(-1)}=\\frac{0.510}{1.86}=0.274.\n\\]\n\nHence the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{m_{AC}}=-3.65\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-0.255=-3.65\\,(x+0.07).\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{0.86+5.2}{2},\\;\\frac{0.510+0}{2}\\Bigr)=\\Bigl(3.03,\\;0.255\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand is perpendicular to \\(AD\\).  \nThe slope of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{AD}= \\frac{0.510-0}{0.86-5.2}= \\frac{0.510}{-4.34}= -0.1175,\n\\]\n\nso the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(+\\dfrac{1}{0.1175}=8.51\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-0.255=8.51\\,(x-3.03).\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (1) and (2) gives the intersection point  \n\n\\[\nP\\approx(2.25,\\;-4.69).\n\\]\n\n(Exact coordinates are unnecessary; the numerical values are sufficient for the argument that follows.)\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles)  \n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A(0.86,0.510)\\) and \\(P(2.25,-4.69)\\); its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{-4.69-0.510}{2.25-0.86}= \\frac{-5.20}{1.39}= -3.74 .\n\\]\n\nHence the equation of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-0.510=-3.74\\,(x-0.86).\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(y\\) from (3) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=1\\) yields a quadratic equation whose two solutions are \\(x=0.86\\) (point \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}\\approx 0.95 .\n\\]\n\nCorrespondingly  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}= -3.74\\,(0.95-0.86)+0.510\\approx -0.313 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE\\approx(0.95,\\,-0.313).\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nSubstituting (3) into 