ough \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) at the point where the invariant line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\).  \n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one.  \nDenote their intersection points by \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) (\\(A\\neq B\\)).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line.  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect in the two points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non‑trivial spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBy definition of a spiral similarity,\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(C)=D,\\qquad \\sigma(M)=N,\\qquad \\sigma(E)=F,\\qquad \\sigma(B)=B,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand every point of the ray \\(AP\\) is fixed; in particular the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(P\\)\n\nFrom (3) the circle  \n\n\\[\n\\omega:=\\{X\\mid PX=PA\\}\n\\]\n\npasses through \\(A,C,D\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) maps the ordered triple \\((A,C,M)\\) to \\((A,D,N)\\) and preserves oriented angles, the image of the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) under \\(\\sigma\\) is the unique circle through \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) having the same oriented angle subtended by the chord \\(AD\\) as \\(\\Omega\\) does at \\(M\\); that circle is precisely \\(\\omega\\).  \nApplying \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) gives the same conclusion for \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\Omega)=\\sigma(\\Gamma)=\\omega .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nApply \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma^{-1}(P)=P\\) (the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is fixed), we obtain a triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle P M' N',\\qquad M'=\\sigma^{-1}(M),\\; N'=\\sigma^{-1}(N).\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(M',N'\\in AP\\) and \\(AM'=AN,\\; AN'=AM\\).  \nLet \\(H'\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M' N'\\).  \nSince a similarity sends orthocentres to orthocentres,  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(H')=H .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle P M' N'\\) the circum‑centre is \\(P\\); therefore the line joining vertex \\(P\\) to the circum‑centre, i.e. the line \\(AP\\), is the **\\(P\\)-symmedian** of this triangle (a standard fact: the line through a vertex and the circum‑centre is the internal symmedian issued from that vertex).\n\nThe \\(P\\)-altitude of \\(\\triangle P M' N'\\) is the line through its orthocentre \\(H'\\) parallel to the \\(P\\)-symmedian, hence parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nApplying the similarity \\(\\sigma\\) we obtain:\n\n* the image of the \\(P\\)-altitude is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);\n* the image of the \\(P\\)-symmedian is the **\\(A\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BMN\\) (because \\(\\sigma\\) sends the vertices \\(M',N'\