ius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution. The key step is to show that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nSince the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), this equality is exactly the condition for that line to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n1.  Observe that \\(MN\\perp AB\\) and that the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently  \n\n    \\[\n    PH\\parallel AB .\n    \\]\n\n2.  Because \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) and \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\), the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)).  \n\n3.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n    \\[\n    \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB .\n    \\]\n\n    The tangent–chord theorem tells us that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) iff  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\n    \\tag{1}\n    \\]\n\n4.  Prove (1).  The crucial observation is that the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) which sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\(\\gamma\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) passes through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  The spiral similarity therefore sends \\(\\gamma\\) to itself; consequently it sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and preserves the angle that these chords subtend at the points where they meet the other circle.  Hence  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\n    \\tag{2}\n    \\]\n\n    A short computation using the law of sines in \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle BFA\\) confirms (2) without invoking any metric equalities.\n\n5.  From (2) we obtain (1), and therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement follows.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points being ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line.  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of the orthocentre\n\nSince the centres \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and the circles intersect, the common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause of (3), \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); this bisector is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  Hence the foot of this altitude is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), and the altitude itself is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\).  Since the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on every altitude, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nDenote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because of (5), \\(\\ell\\) is also parallel to the radical axis \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  By the tangent–chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(E\\in AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(F\\in AP\\), so \\(\\angle BFE=\\angle BFA\\).  Substituting (7) into (6) we see that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\;}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem reduces to proving (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  This similarity sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  In particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(B)=B .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\(\\gamma\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) passes through \\(A\\), \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the three points \\(A,C,D\\) of \\(\\gamma\\) onto the three points \\(A,D,C\\) respectively; consequently \\(\\mathcal S\\) leaves the circle \\(\\gamma\\) invariant (it merely interchanges the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on \\(\\gamma\\)).  Hence \\(\\mathcal S\\) also sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) while preserving the angle that each chord subtends at the other circle.\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the inscribed angle \\(\\angle AEB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  The image of the chord \\(AE\\) is the chord \\(AF\\); the image of the chord \\(EB\\) is the chord \\(FB\\) (because \\(\\mathcal S(B)=B\\)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquation (10) is exactly (8).  Hence (8) holds, and by the discussion of §2 the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent–chord condition (6); consequently it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
