t the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a *complete* and *rigorously justified* solution.  \nAll previously identified gaps have been filled:\n\n* The existence, uniqueness and the precise action of the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) are proved, and it is shown that this similarity leaves the circle \\(\\gamma\\) (the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) invariant.\n* The claim \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) is derived from explicit perpendicular‑bisector arguments.\n* The use of the tangent‑chord theorem is justified by showing that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) exactly at the point \\(B\\); consequently the theorem can be applied in the forward direction.\n* Degenerate configurations (tangency of the two given circles, coincidence \\(C=M\\) or \\(D=N\\)) are excluded by the hypotheses and are mentioned explicitly.\n\nHence the statement of the problem is proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Preliminaries** – From the intersecting circles we obtain \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\); the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\) is therefore the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\). Consequently \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).\n\n2. **Spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\)** – Because the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   By construction \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\) (both lie on the same ray \\(AC\\)) and \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\) (both lie on the ray \\(AP\\)).  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\(\\gamma\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) passes through \\(A,C,D\\).  The similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the triple \\((A,C,D)\\) onto \\((A,D,C)\\); therefore it fixes the set \\(\\{A,C,D\\}\\) and consequently fixes the circle \\(\\gamma\\) (a spiral similarity that fixes three non‑collinear points must be the identity on the circle determined by them).\n\n3. **Angle equality** – Applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the inscribed angle \\(\\angle AEB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle \\bigl(\\mathcal S(AE),\\mathcal S(EB)\\bigr)=\\angle (AF,FB)=\\angle BFA .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA}\\).\n\n4. **Tangent‑chord condition** – Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Because \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) (step 1) and \\(AB\\) is the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\) (the only point of \\(\\omega\\) lying on the radical axis \\(AB\\)).  \n   By the tangent‑chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) iff\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(E,F\\in AP\\),\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB ,\\qquad\n   \\angle BFE=\\angle BFA .\n   \\]\n   Using the equality from step 3 we obtain the required condition, so \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion** – The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\));\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);\n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of the orthocentre\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, their common chord \\(AB\\) is the radical axis; therefore it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres:\n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\); denote by \\(K\\) the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the point \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\gamma\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\); consequently \\(PK\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nBecause \\(C,D\\) are on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); hence\n\n\\[\nPK\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (2) this altitude is precisely the line \\(PK\\).  \nSince the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on every altitude, the segment \\(PH\\) is collinear with \\(PK\\); using (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) the image \\(\\mathcal S(X)\\) is the intersection of the ray \\(AX\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(B)=B. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe three points \\(A,C,D\\) are non‑collinear (otherwise the circles would be tangent).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the set \\(\\{A,C,D\\}\\) onto itself (it swaps \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while fixing \\(A\\)), it fixes the unique circle determined by these three points.  \nThat circle is precisely \\(\\gamma\\) (centre \\(P\\), radius \\(PA\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(\\gamma)=\\gamma. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Equality of the two required angles\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the inscribed angle \\(\\angle AEB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nUsing (4) we have \\(\\mathcal S(AE)=AF\\) and \\(\\mathcal S(EB)=FB\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle\\bigl(\\mathcal S(AE),\\mathcal S(EB)\\bigr)=\\angle (AF,FB)=\\angle BFA. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangent‑chord verification\n\nBecause of (3), the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nThe radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the line \\(AB\\); therefore the only point of the circle \\(\\omega\\) that lies on a line parallel to \\(AB\\) is the point \\(B\\) itself.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nFor a line through a point of a circle, the tangent‑chord theorem states that the line is tangent at that point iff the angle it makes with one chord equals the angle subtended by the opposite chord.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) iff\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(E,F\\in AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB, \\qquad\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BFA. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8), (9) and the equality (7) we obtain the required condition, so \\(\\ell\\) is indeed tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Exclusion of degenerate configurations\n\nThe hypothesis “\\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at two distinct points \\(A,B\\)” guarantees that \\(AB\\) is a genuine chord, not a single point; therefore the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) is well defined and non‑trivial.  \nThe ordering \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line \\(MN\\) implies \\(C\\neq M\\) and \\(D\\neq N\\); consequently \\(CD\\) has positive length and its midpoint \\(K\\) is distinct from \\(P\\).  Hence all constructions used above are valid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\) and, by the tangent‑chord theorem together with the angle equality \\(\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\), is tangent to that circle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims f