he radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) indeed meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\); consequently it is tangent to that circle at \\(B\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Pre‑liminaries.**  \n   * From the intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we have \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\).  \n   * Using the fact that \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear we obtain  \n     \\[\n     PH\\parallel AB\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad BH\\parallel AP .\\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   * There is a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * By construction \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\) (both lie on the ray \\(AC\\)) and \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\) (both lie on the ray \\(AP\\)).  \n   * Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the three non‑collinear points \\(A,C,D\\), it fixes the circum‑circle \\(\\gamma\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); in particular \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).\n\n3. **Angle equalities.**  \n   * Applying \\(\\