ing their centres is the radical axis of the two circles; consequently the common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\):\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PC\\) equals \\(PD\\) and the line \\(P\\!-\\!{\\rm midpoint}(CD)\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nBut \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is a line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nHence\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\n\\]\nTogether with (3) we obtain\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the quadrilateral \\(ABMN\\).  From (3) we have \\(\\angle ABM=\\angle ABN=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the points \\(A,B,M,N\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(AB\\).  \nConsequently the opposite angles of this cyclic quadrilateral are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle AMB=\\angle ANB .\n\\]\nSince \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the above equality yields\n\\[\n\\angle AMN=\\angle ANM .\n\\]\nThus \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through the midpoint of \\(AB\\) that is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nBecause \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) by (4), the reflection of \\(M\\) in the line \\(PH\\) is \\(N\\).  \nTherefore the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (which is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) is also the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\); consequently the other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are symmetric with respect to this line.  \nFrom this symmetry we deduce that the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AP\\):\n\\[\nBH\\parallel AP .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\