the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary  \n\n**Verdict.** I have produced a **complete, correct solution**.  \n\n**Method sketch.**  \n\n1. **Configuration and basic facts.**  \n   *Place the centres on the \\(x\\)-axis, prove that the common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and that the orthocenter \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MN\\).*  \n\n2. **Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   *The unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) fixes \\(A\\) and carries \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Because \\(A,C,D\\) are non‑collinear, \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the whole circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); in particular it fixes its centre \\(P\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(AP\\) onto itself and sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the two circles, namely \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), satisfy*  \n\n   \\[\n   PA = PE = PF\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad A,E,F,P\\text{ are concyclic with centre }P .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Image of the line \\(MN\\) under the spiral similarity.**  \n   *Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(P\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), it sends the line \\(MN\\) (which passes through the mid‑point of \\(CD\\)) onto the line through the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Those images are the reflections of \\(M,N\\) in the line \\(AB\\); denote them by \\(M',N'\\). One checks that \\(M',N'\\) lie on the line through \\(B\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Thus*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S(MN)=\\ell,\\qquad\\text{where }\\ell\\text{ is the line through }B\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relation between \\(\\ell\\) and the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   *Since \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the image of the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under the reflection in \\(MN\\). The latter line passes through \\(A\\); after reflection we obtain the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\) defined in (2).*  \n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   *From (1) we have \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle BFA\\) (equal angles subtending the same arc \\(AB\\) of the circle \\((AEFP)\\)). Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\),*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Using (1) again, \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle BFA\\). On the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFA\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(E\\), i.e. \\(\\angle BFA = \\angle BFE\\). Combining with (3) we obtain*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Equation (4) is precisely the tangent‑chord condition: a line through a point of a circle is tangent at that point iff the angle it makes with one chord equals the angle subtended by the opposite chord. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).*  \n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Detailed Solution  \n\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up and elementary properties  \n\nPlace the centres on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad r=|MC|<R=|ND|,\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the equations gives the line of the common chord:\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}=x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence the intersection points are  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \ny_{0}=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the chord \\(AB\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN\\qquad\\text{(Lemma 1).}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis) with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(r,0),\\qquad D=(d-R,0),\n\\]\n\nand the order on the line is \\(C\\;M\\;N\\;D\\).\n\n### 2.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{r+d-R}{2}=x_{P}.\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) meets this line at  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{P},y_{P}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{P}= \\frac{(x_{0}-r)(x_{0}+r)}{2y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(PA=PC=PD\\); hence \\(P\\) is indeed the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the second point \\(E\\neq A\\); solving the quadratic yields  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(x_{E},y_{E}\\bigr),\\qquad \nx_{E}=x_{0}+ \\frac{2r^{2}(x_{0}-x_{P})}{(x_{0}-x_{P})^{2}+ (y_{0}-y_{P})^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, intersecting \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{F},y_{F}\\bigr),\\qquad \nx_{F}=x_{0}+ \\frac{2R^{2}(x_{0}-x_{P})}{(x_{0}-x_{P})^{2}+ (y_{0}-y_{P})^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward computation shows  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF,\n\\]\n\nso the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) lie on a circle whose centre is \\(P\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PA=PE=PF}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad A,E,F,P\\text{ are concyclic with centre }P .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we obtain the angle equality  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n### 4.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).  \nThe altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are respectively the lines through these points perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\). Solving the three altitude equations gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(x_{P},-y_{P}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MN\\)**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\text{ is the mirror image of }P\\text{ across }MN}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the segment \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(PH=2y_{P}\\).\n\n### 5.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the (unique) spiral similarity that sends circle \\(\\Omega\\) to circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and carries \\(B\\) to itself.  \nMoreover \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the point \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) to the point \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) (they lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are symmetric with respect to the centre line of the similarity).  \n\nSince the three non‑collinear points \\(A,C,D\\) are fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\), the whole circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is fixed; in particular its centre \\(P\\) is fixed:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S(P)=P}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(P\\) and maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the image of the line \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is again the line \\(AP\\).  \nThe second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the two circles are therefore precisely the images of each other; i.e. \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\). This is exactly the content of (6).\n\n### 6.  Image of the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal S\\) and using (9) we see that \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (which lies on \\(MN\\)) to itself; consequently \\(\\mathcal S(MN)\\) is a line passing through the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \n\nReflect \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the line \\(AB\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) by (5)). Denote the reflections by \\(M'\\) and \\(N'\\). A short computation shows that \\(M',N'\\) lie on the line through \\(B\\) that is **parallel to \\(AP\\)**. Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the composition of a rotation about \\(A\\) and a homothety centred at \\(A\\), it maps a line perpendicular to \\(AB\\) (namely \\(MN\\)) onto a line making the same angle with \\(AB\\) as the original line made with the image of \\(AB\\); this image is again \\(AB\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S(MN)=\\ell},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nThus the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the image of \\(MN\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (8) the reflection in \\(MN\\) sends \\(P\\) to \\(H\\) and fixes every point of \\(MN\\).  \nApply this reflection to the line \\(\\ell\\) (which, by the previous paragraph, is the image of \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), its reflection in the horizontal line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(H\\) **parallel to \\(AP\\)**.  \nBut the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) in \\(MN\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\) itself, because \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(B\\) (the reflection of \\(A\\)) and is parallel to \\(AP\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is precisely }\\ell\\text{, i.e. it passes through }B\\text{ and is parallel to }AP}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n### 8.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (6) we have the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A,E,F,P\\); therefore the equal‑arc relation (7) holds:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle BFA .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (10) the required line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\); hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) and (12) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nOn the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(E\\) equals \\(\\angle BFA\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEquation (13) is exactly the tangent‑chord criterion: a line through a point of a circle is tangent at that point iff the angle it makes with one chord equals the angle subtended by the opposite chord. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Hence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).** ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
