\\)\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the (unique) spiral similarity that sends the\ncircle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(A\\).\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the similarity also\nfixes \\(B\\).\n\n*The similarity sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).*  \nIndeed, \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is the common\nperpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two centres; the\ndefinition of \\(\\mathcal S\\) (rotation about \\(A\\) followed by a\nhomothety centred at \\(A\\)) maps a point of \\(\\Omega\\) on that line to\nthe corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) on the same line, i.e. \\(C\\mapsto\nD\\).\n\nSince the three non‑collinear points \\(A,C,D\\) are fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\),\nthe whole circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is fixed; in particular its\ncentre \\(P\\) is fixed:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S(P)=P}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(P\\) and maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the\nimage of the line \\(AP\\) is again the line \\(AP\\).  Hence the second\nintersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is carried to the second\nintersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\mathcal S(E)=F }. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain the well‑known cyclicity\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PA=PE=PF},\\qquad\\text{so }A,E,F,P\\text{ lie on a circle centred at }P.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(B\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\nReflect \\(\\ell\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \nBecause the reflection sends \\(H\\) to \\(P\\) (by (1)) and fixes the\ndirection of the line (parallelism is preserved), the reflected line is\nexactly the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\)\nitself.\n\nThus the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) in \\(MN\\) is the line \\(AP\\).  Since the\nreflection of a line through a point of a line is the line through the\nreflected point with the same direction, the original line \\(\\ell\\) is\nthe image of \\(AP\\) under the reflection in \\(MN\\).\n\nBut the reflection in \\(MN\\) sends the point \\(A\\) to its mirror image,\nwhich, by Section 2, is the point \\(B\\).  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) passes\nthrough \\(B\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\ell\\text{ is the line through }B\\text{ parallel to }AP }.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (4) the quadrilateral \\(A\\,E\\,F\\,P\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); hence\nthe equal‑arc relation\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle BFA . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (5) the angle that the line \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord\n\\(BE\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the same chord:\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6) and (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nOn the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the angle subtended by the chord \\(BF\\) at\nthe point \\(E\\) is exactly \\(\\angle BFA\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) is the **tangent‑chord theorem**: a line through a point\n\\(B\\) of a circle is tangent at \\(B\\) iff the angle it makes with one\nchord (\\(BE\\)) equals the angle subtended by the opposite chord\n(\\(BF\\)).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(B\\) parallel to\n\\(AP\\); this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n∎"
