 line \\(MN\\) is the common radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently \\(PM=PN\\).  \n\nThus the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nThe other two altitudes are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\) (because the configuration is symmetric under reflection in \\(MN\\)).  \nTherefore the orthocentre \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MN\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MN\\text{ bisects }PH . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of the two circles; under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) it is mapped onto the line joining the images of the two intersection points of the circles with \\(MN\\).  \nThose images are precisely the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles, i.e. the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(MN)=EF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(P\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (which lies on \\(MN\\)) is sent to the midpoint of \\(EF\\); consequently the line \\(EF\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nReflect the line \\(EF\\) in the axis \\(MN\\).  \nBy (2) the reflection sends \\(P\\) to \\(H\\) and leaves every point of \\(MN\\) fixed; therefore the reflected line passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to the original line \\(AP\\) (the image of \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is itself).  \nDenote this reflected line by \\(\\ell\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the reflection of \\(EF\\) in \\(MN\\) interchanges the two intersection points of \\(EF\\) with the circle \\((BEF)\\), the reflected line meets \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) with (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nOn the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(E\\) equals \\(\\angle BFA\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquality (6) is exactly the tangent‑chord criterion: a line through a point of a circle is tangent at that point iff the angle it makes with one chord equals the angle subtended by the opposite chord. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required tangency is proved. ∎"
